Plan (read-only, no build). Facts from his b3 log (/downloads/Untitled-f8946219.txt): adapters line = "Intel(R) UHD Graphics; NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 6GB Laptop GPU", 12 CPU threads. Caveat: that line comes from the registry and its order is NOT necessarily DirectML's order, so "adapter 0 = Intel" is likely but unproven. Sourced: ORT DML docs say device_id is the IDXGIFactory::EnumAdapters index, 0 is "the default adapter, typically the primary display GPU" (https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/execution-providers/DirectML-ExecutionProvider.html), and an ORT maintainer reply says on multi-GPU machines id 0 is typically the integrated GPU and 1 the discrete card, "depending" on the machine (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/10606; also https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/14994, https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/discussions/14840). Our code never sets device_id, so it uses 0. Another sourced hazard: DirectML on an Intel iGPU in an Optimus laptop gave different results from CPU/NVIDIA (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/14214), so the iGPU may also be less accurate, not only slower. The ort crate we use supports DirectMLExecutionProvider::default().with_device_id(n) (in its source, execution_providers/directml.rs line 40).
Evidence in his log consistent with an iGPU: det run 0.25-0.47 s but rec 1.5-2.7 s per call, an integrated GPU doing 64x48x320 and 8x48x1280 batches. Not proof (could also be CPU-fallback ops), see "what proves it" below.

Plan, in order:
1. Adapter enumeration through DXGI (raw COM, no new crates): CreateDXGIFactory1 -> EnumAdapters1(i) until not found -> GetDesc1: description, VendorId (0x10DE NVIDIA, 0x1002 AMD, 0x8086 Intel), DedicatedVideoMemory, software flag. Log one line per adapter: "DXGI adapter 0: name, vendor, VRAM MB". This also answers the question "which one did DirectML use": it uses the same index as device_id. (Inference: ORT enumerates in this same order; I'd confirm by the benchmark below, not assume.)
2. Candidate list = non-software adapters, discrete first (largest dedicated VRAM, vendor NVIDIA/AMD), the iGPU last. Explicit device_id passed to the session builder (probe session and the fixed sessions) so the worker never silently uses adapter 0.
3. Per-adapter micro-benchmark at worker start, on a real recognizer shape (e.g. 64x48x320 and 8x48x1280): create a session on each candidate adapter, run 1 warm + 2 timed calls, log "bench adapter i name: create X ms, run Y ms/call", and run the same on CPU (all threads). Pick the fastest including CPU; log "using ...". Cache the winner (adapter name + driver description) in settings.json so the next job skips the benchmark (re-run if the adapter list changes). Cost: about 1-3 s once per job on first use.
4. Watchdog stays (already worked: it measured CPU 4.7 s vs GPU 7.9 s and switched), but it should not just drop to CPU: if another GPU adapter exists that was not benchmarked, try that first.
5. If every GPU adapter loses to CPU, default to CPU with the tuned CPU path (see 6) and say so in ocr.log. DML stays an option, not the default.
6. CPU path improvements (his CPU is the real fallback, 12 threads: 4.7 s/page on that page, 2-6 s later): (a) thread count: try physical cores/2-3 workers each with fewer intra-op threads instead of one session using 12 (the oar-ocr PR measured big gains from thread-pool tuning on Windows: https://github.com/GreatV/oar-ocr/pull/165), i.e. pages in parallel across 2-3 worker processes (what ocrmypdf does with --jobs: https://ocrmypdf.readthedocs.io/en/stable/performance.html), at ~0.5-0.9 GB RAM each; (b) smaller detector long side is cheap (det is small), recognition dominates: 64 calls for 492 rows on page 30 means bucket/batch mix is fine, the recognizer FLOPs are the cost; (c) later: int8 recognizer (ORT quantization, https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/performance/model-optimizations/quantization.html), needs an accuracy test, so not first.
7. Also log per worker: ORT DML device id used, driver description, and whether session creation took the same time per adapter.

What log lines would prove each cause:
- iGPU used: "DXGI adapter 0: Intel..." plus bench lines where adapter 0 is slow and adapter 1 (NVIDIA) fast; Task Manager GPU graph on the Intel GPU during OCR would also show it.
- DML ops falling back to CPU: fast detector but slow rec on the NVIDIA adapter too (bench run ms/call for 64x48x320 well above ~100 ms), plus litepdf.exe CPU % high during GPU OCR. ORT can print which nodes are assigned to which EP at verbose log level (I can add an optional env switch to dump this, unverified how readable it is).
- Memory-pattern/arena settings: A/B with those two options removed, one extra bench line. Low prior; sourced note: https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/20983.
- GPU context/driver: bench create time per adapter.

Open questions for the user (only he can answer): Task Manager > Performance during OCR, does GPU 0 (Intel) or GPU 1 (NVIDIA) show activity? And Windows Settings > System > Display > Graphics: does litepdf.exe have a "High performance" preference? Not needed to start the build, but handy to confirm.
Time estimate for build + CPU-proxy tests: about 30-40 min, DXGI code can only be compiled and checked for sanity under Wine (it reports only the Wine adapter), real adapter selection stays unverified until his PC.
